  "articleBody": "Remodeling can be exciting, but some common plumbing coordination mistakes can really throw a wrench in your plans. Here are a few to steer clear of: \n\n1. Missing schedule conflicts: It's easy to assume everyone will just work around each other, but plumbing needs clear access. How to avoid it: Insist on a detailed schedule that shows how each trade's work will flow together.\n\n2. Ordering trim or fixtures before valve compatibility is confirmed: You might fall in love with a faucet, but if it doesn't match the rough-in valve, you're in for a costly surprise. How to avoid it: Confirm all fixture and valve selections with your plumber before purchasing anything.\n\n3. Failing to communicate changes that affect the plumbing scope: A small change in a cabinet layout could mean a big change for plumbing lines. How to avoid it: Keep open communication with your contractor and plumber about any design changes, no matter how small.\n\n4. Not having a clear lead on coordination: When everyone's responsible, no one's responsible. How to avoid it: Ensure your general contractor, like Abraham at HH Remodeling LLC, is actively managing and coordinating all the sub-trades.\n\n5. Forgetting about inspections and permits: These aren't optional and can cause significant delays if not planned for. How to avoid it: Work with a professional who handles all necessary permits and inspections as part of the project timeline.",
